sue one path.\n\nCompare:\n- Pure Straight (16 points) if you get [W7,W8,W9]\n- Or Mixed Triple Chow if you get [T7,T8,T9], but you already have two Character chows, so adding Bamboo chow won't give Mixed Triple Chow (requires three chows in different suits with same numbers).\n\nNot applicable.\n\nSo Pure Straight is good.\n\nTo complete it, you need W7 or W9.\n\nYou have W8.\n\nSo wait for W7 or W9.\n\nIn hand, you have W1,W2,W3,W8 \u2014 so discarding W1,W2, or W3 breaks the lower chow, but since you have all three, it doesn't matter.\n\nW1 is less central than W2 or W3 for waits, but for chow, no difference.\n\nSince you are going for [W7,W8,W9], the lower ones are less critical.\n\nBut you still need [W1,W2,W3] for the pure straight.\n\nSo keep them.\n\nBut you must discard something.\n\nAmong the options, the most dispensable is J2, because:\n- It's a single honor\n- Dragons are risky to keep\n- You have no other J2, so no pair\n- Not helping any immediate meld\n\nWhereas T8 and T9 are key to flexibility.\n\nSimilarly, B3 and B9 are potential terminals.\n\nW1, W2, W3 are part of a chow.\n\nW8 is needed for [W7,W8,W9].\n\nSo best discard: J2.\n\nIt has no synergy, and is potentially dangerous (someone might win off it).\n\nMoreover, Dragon Pung is only 2 points, and you don't have it.\n\nSo discard J2.\n\nReason: To pursue Pure Straight (16 points), preserve W1,W2,W3,W8,T8,T9; J2 is a singleton honor with no immediate value and poses a risk if discarded late. Discarding it now improves safety and focuses on the high-probability Pure Straight with Characters.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
